{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"TWO MORE HYPOTHESES ELIMINATED, and one CONSTRAINT ON THE TEST DESIGN discovered. Both cheap, both recorded so the search keeps narrowing instead of circling.\n\n**ELIMINATED — the v3 migration strips the declarations.** The offline harness (`tests/unit/workflow/test_code_review_workflow.py:402`) runs the gate through `migrate.migrate_to_current(yaml.safe_load(gate))` before execution, so a migration that dropped or relocated `model:` would explain everything. VERIFIED it does not: before and after migration the doc is identical — base `model=frontier`; round_a/round_b `model_ladder=['frontier']`; verify/coach `model=standard`. So the declarations survive YAML -> migrate -> doc intact.\n\n**CONSTRAINT ON THE END-TO-END TEST — this changes how AC 'recording runner' must be built.** The existing offline harness passes `agent_runner=_FakeRunner(...)`, which REPLACES `RunnerAgentStep` wholesale. So a naive recording runner in that style would observe `ctx.step['model']` (the declared token delivered by the engine) but would NEVER exercise `RunnerAgentStep`'s `resolve_model(cfg, step=ctx.step.get('model'), ...)` — the exact line whose behaviour is in question. A test built that way would pass while proving only what 3768's tests already proved.\n=> the recording seam must sit BENEATH `RunnerAgentStep`, not instead of it: keep the production agent step and inject a fake UNDERLYING runner that captures the `cfg.model` it is handed. That observes the post-resolution value, which is the fact at issue. Write it that way, and confirm RED.\n\nREMAINING SUSPECT, stated as a suspect and not a conclusion: `config.py:211` is `return resolve_model_string(step or workflow or cfg.model)` — it passes NO `repo_root`, so the class table is loaded from the ambient root. The live code-review run executes inside `gate_source.gate_read_root(handle)`, which redirects reads to a snapshot. Whether that redirect changes what the class table resolves to is UNVERIFIED — and note it cannot be the whole story, because a fallback class table would still give `verify` (declaring `standard`) a SONNET model, and the live log showed OPUS. Step 0's per-step attribution is what settles this; do not act on this suspicion before then.\n\nCUMULATIVE ELIMINATIONS on this defect: config-not-loaded (H2), class-table-fallback (H3), migration-strips-declarations, doc-not-my-copy, review_code-does-not-run-the-gate. All ruled out by experiment."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785615343111356001,"uuid":"9afbf697-56d4-4694-8253-45bd5d8e578a"}